/*
 * The devicetree overlay for this step — same for all of Chapter 4.
 *
 * The DK's devicetree already wires a SECOND UART, &uart1, to pins
 * P1.01 (RX) and P1.02 (TX) — the board even labels it
 * "arduino_serial", because those pins land on the Arduino-style
 * headers. But the SoC ships uart1 DISABLED. Enabling it is our
 * job, here.
 *
 * What is NOT here, deliberately: the baud rate. The LD2410 speaks
 * at 256,000 baud — a speed Zephyr's devicetree binding for the
 * nRF UARTE does not list (its standard-speed table jumps from
 * 250,000 to 460,800, and the build refuses anything else). The
 * hardware divider can generate 256,000 almost exactly, though,
 * so the application sets it at run time, from the datasheet
 * formula — read the comment above ld2410_apply_baud() in
 * main.c; it is the most honest three lines in this chapter.
 *
 * The console (your logs) stays on uart0 / the USB virtual COM
 * port, untouched. Sensor on uart1, logs on uart0: never let a
 * peripheral you are debugging share the wire you debug through.
 */

&uart1 {
	status = "okay";
};
